"""Editorial review of the supplied Howlett book; keys are not blindly trusted.
Groups share an explanation only when they test the same architectural distinction.
"""
REVIEW = {}
def group(ids, task, topic, reason, ref, status='Reviewed', key=None):
    for n in ids:
        if n in REVIEW: raise ValueError(f'Duplicate review for {n}')
        REVIEW[n]=dict(task=task,topic=topic,reason=reason,ref=ref,status=status,key=key)

group([1,150],'1.2','encrypted hybrid tunnels','Site-to-Site VPN establishes IPsec tunnels between an on-premises network and a VPC over existing network connectivity. Direct Connect instead provisions a dedicated link; VPC peering connects VPCs, and Route 53 handles DNS.','VPN')
group([2,8],'1.2','subnet-level network filtering','Network ACLs apply stateless, ordered allow and deny rules at subnet boundaries. They can explicitly deny a source CIDR. Security groups instead provide stateful allow rules at resource network interfaces.','NACL')
group([3],'1.1','temporary role credentials','IAM roles define assumable permissions and provide temporary security credentials. They do not eliminate credentials: AWS SDKs can retrieve and refresh role credentials without embedding long-lived keys. The supplied option must be corrected.','IAM','Corrected')
group([4,17,50,75,86],'1.3','S3 encryption at rest','S3 server-side encryption protects object data at rest. New S3 uploads are already encrypted with SSE-S3 by default; an explicit bucket configuration can choose SSE-KMS when key control is required. Networking and versioning do not perform encryption.','S3Encryption')
group([5,35,90],'1.2','DDoS mitigation','AWS Shield provides DDoS protections. Shield Standard is automatic, while Shield Advanced adds paid capabilities. WAF focuses on HTTP request rules, and monitoring or access policies do not substitute for DDoS mitigation.','Shield')
group([6],'1.1','S3 principal authorization','A bucket policy can grant or deny specified principals access to the bucket and its objects. The effective decision also depends on other applicable policies and explicit denies; merely adding an allow does not remove every other principal\'s permissions.','S3Policy')
group([7,80],'1.2','private AWS service endpoints','VPC endpoints let eligible services be reached privately without assigning public IPs to the calling instances. The endpoint type and supported service determine routing. Endpoints still require IAM authorization and do not themselves guarantee encryption.','Endpoint')
group([9],'1.2','automatic DDoS protection','The included baseline protection is AWS Shield Standard, not the optional paid Shield Advanced tier. CloudFront and Route 53 benefit from AWS\'s built-in DDoS protections. The supplied option name is corrected while retaining its letter.','Shield','Corrected')
group([10],'1.3','KMS key-material rotation','Enable automatic rotation for eligible KMS keys. KMS keeps old key material for decrypting earlier ciphertext. Eligibility and configurable rotation behavior depend on the key type; an IAM role or alarm does not rotate key material.','KMSRotation')
group([11,32,36,79,96,299],'1.2','AWS API auditing','CloudTrail records supported AWS API activity, including caller information, actions and request details. Configure the needed management/data events and durable trails for the required coverage. It is not a packet capture or a guarantee that every application action is logged.','CloudTrail')
group([12,44,66,92],'1.3','transport encryption','TLS encrypts and integrity-protects supported client/service connections. Network isolation and IAM permissions complement TLS but do not encrypt bytes in transit by themselves. Endpoints and clients must actually use the secure protocol.','TLS')
group([13,31,41,61],'1.3','managed encryption keys','KMS manages cryptographic keys and integrates with services such as DynamoDB for encryption at rest. Secrets Manager stores secret values, ACM handles certificates, and WAF filters web requests. Encryption does not replace access authorization.','KMS')
group([14,23,42,59,64,82],'1.1','identity authorization','IAM policies express allowed or denied actions on resources, subject to conditions and policy evaluation. Roles carry permissions and are assumed by trusted principals. Network controls cannot substitute for resource/action authorization.','IAM')
group([15,37],'1.1','stronger console authentication','MFA adds another authentication factor beyond a password. It reduces the risk from a stolen password, but users still need legitimate identities and appropriate permissions. Federation and roles can be part of the design rather than competing replacements for MFA.','MFA')
group([16],'1.2','configuration compliance evaluation','AWS Config rules evaluate resource configurations against desired compliance requirements. Inspector specializes in supported workload vulnerabilities; GuardDuty detects threats; CloudTrail records API history. Define appropriate Config rules rather than expecting every security standard to be evaluated automatically.','Config')
group([18,51,71,88],'1.2','aggregated security posture','Security Hub aggregates supported security findings and provides security posture/compliance views across integrated services and accounts. It does not replace the source detectors or automatically remediate every finding.','SecurityHub')
group([19,21,47,72],'1.2','stateful resource traffic filtering','Security groups allow specified protocols, ports and source/destination CIDRs or security groups at network interfaces. They are stateful and have no explicit deny rules; omit unwanted sources and audit all attached groups.','SG')
group([20,43,57],'1.2','configuration checks and remediation','Config rules identify noncompliant resource configurations and can invoke configured remediation. Config is primarily detective; it is not a blanket preventive permission system. The practice wording explicitly asks for evaluation and configured remediation.','Config','Corrected')
group([22,77],'1.3','TLS certificate management','ACM manages supported SSL/TLS certificates for integrated endpoints. A certificate must be attached to the endpoint and a TLS connection used; obtaining a certificate alone does not encrypt every network connection.','ACM')
group([24],'1.2','application secret rotation','Secrets Manager stores protected application secrets and supports configured rotation. Parameter Store SecureString is also valid for secure values, so the practice question adds a managed-rotation requirement to distinguish the choices.','Secrets','Corrected')
group([25,46,74],'1.1','bucket authorization ambiguity','Both identity-based IAM policies/roles and S3 bucket policies can authorize access. A bucket policy is a resource policy, not the only possible method. The original wording does not specify a cross-account or resource-policy requirement, so it does not support declaring every alternative wrong.','S3Policy','Review only')
group([26,48,69,73,87],'1.2','threat detection','GuardDuty analyzes supported telemetry for suspicious behavior and generates findings. Investigation and response are separate operational steps and can be automated through integrations; the service does not automatically fix every threat.','GuardDuty')
group([27,39,60,63,83,94,100],'1.2','HTTP attack filtering','AWS WAF inspects supported web traffic and applies request rules to mitigate attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting. It complements secure coding and is not a guarantee that every exploit will be prevented.','WAF')
group([28,70],'1.1','organization permission guardrails','Organizations SCPs bound maximum permissions for principals in affected member accounts. They do not grant permissions and do not restrict the management account in the same way. Local administrator allows cannot override applicable SCP denies.','Organizations')
group([29,68],'1.1','credential hygiene','Key rotation limits the lifetime of exposed long-term keys but cannot ensure credentials are never leaked. MFA protects interactive authentication; assumed roles reduce long-lived key distribution. The source overstates guarantees and offers overlapping security practices.','IAM','Review only')
group([30,85],'1.1','multi-account security policy scope','Organizations supports account organization and policy guardrails, but Config can evaluate compliance and WAF policies can govern particular workloads. The broad word compliance does not distinguish preventive authorization from detective resource checks.','Organizations','Review only')
group([33,40,151,239,250,268,276],'1.2','VPC network isolation','A VPC is a logically isolated virtual network with configurable subnets, routes and network security controls. Connectivity services attach networks, while compute services run applications inside network configurations rather than defining that network themselves.','VPC')
group([34,56],'1.3','EBS encryption','Enable encryption for a new EBS volume or enable the regional EBS encryption-by-default setting. Existing unencrypted volumes require an encrypted replacement path; IAM or monitoring does not encrypt an existing volume.','EBS')
group([38],'1.2','VPC network ACL configuration','Network ACLs are configured as VPC networking resources. Calling this centralized management is imprecise: VPC does not itself provide a global organization-wide ACL manager. The practice stem asks where subnet network ACLs are configured.','NACL','Corrected')
group([45,65,93],'1.2','managed secrets','Secrets Manager stores secret values under access controls and encryption and supports configured rotation workflows. Application code must retrieve secrets securely and avoid exposing them in logs or built images.','Secrets')
group([49],'1.3','authorization versus certificate management','ACM manages certificates for TLS endpoints; it does not decide which application principals are authorized to access data. IAM authorizes supported AWS requests. The original statement conflates transport confidentiality, certificate identity and access authorization and has no unique service answer.','ACM','Review only')
group([52],'1.2','IP access-control scope','Security groups restrict network connectivity by source IP, while IAM conditions such as aws:SourceIp can constrain supported API requests. The question does not specify the access plane, so either can be valid in a suitable context.','SG','Review only')
group([53],'1.3','multi-Region data security','Regional KMS keys can protect data at rest, but eligible multi-Region KMS keys also exist. IAM policies are equally necessary to restrict access. Without specifying encryption or threat requirements, no listed option is the sole best data-protection design.','KMS','Review only')
group([54],'1.3','managed RDS backups','AWS Backup centrally schedules and retains supported RDS backups and coordinates supported restores. Native RDS backups are also valid but are not offered here. Define retention and test restores rather than treating backup creation as proof of recovery.','Backup')
group([55],'1.2','Lambda service authorization','A Lambda execution role supplies permissions and temporary credentials for supported AWS service calls. Network endpoints provide connectivity and TLS protects transport; MFA is not an unattended function authentication mechanism. The practice wording explicitly asks about authorized API calls.','IAM','Corrected')
group([58],'1.2','CloudTrail function','CloudTrail records AWS account API activity. Network flow visibility, stored-data encryption, and cryptographic key management are separate functions. Event selection and retention determine what audit history is available.','CloudTrail')
group([62],'1.1','layered data access control','IAM policies and roles, bucket policies, and network controls can all protect different access paths. The broad question does not establish that bucket policies or security groups are wrong. A secure design combines controls according to the resource and threat model.','IAM','Review only')
group([67],'1.3','dedicated cryptographic hardware','CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules and cryptographic operations with customer-controlled key administration. It is not VPC provisioning, network telemetry, or a disaster-recovery orchestrator.','CloudHSM')
group([76],'1.2','configuration and secret parameters','Systems Manager Parameter Store holds configuration values and supports encrypted SecureString parameters. It is not DDoS mitigation or network control. For managed credential rotation requirements, Secrets Manager offers additional capabilities.','ParameterStore')
group([78,180],'3.4','private hybrid connectivity','Direct Connect establishes dedicated private network connectivity between on-premises sites and AWS. VPN provides encrypted tunnels over existing connectivity. Direct Connect is not encrypted by default; additional encryption is needed when required. The practice stem distinguishes the dedicated connection.','DirectConnect','Corrected')
group([81],'1.1','temporary credentials','STS issues temporary security credentials for supported role assumption and federation operations. IAM defines the identities and permissions, but STS is the credential-issuing service named in this question.','STS')
group([84],'1.3','database encryption','RDS encryption at rest protects supported storage and backups, and TLS protects supported database connections. Key permissions and client TLS validation still matter; monitoring and routing are not encryption mechanisms.','RDS')
group([89],'1.2','geographic web filtering','WAF geographic-match rules filter supported HTTP requests by country. They cannot govern access to every kind of AWS resource or API. The practice question is narrowed to a supported web endpoint.','WAF','Corrected')
group([91],'1.3','EFS encryption at rest','Choose encryption at rest for the EFS file system to protect stored data with KMS-backed encryption. In-transit encryption and access controls address separate threats; network rules and metrics do not encrypt stored files.','EFS')
group([95],'1.2','VPC flow telemetry','VPC Flow Logs capture supported metadata about IP traffic at VPC, subnet or interface scope. They are not full packet payload captures. GuardDuty can analyze telemetry, but Flow Logs are the network-flow recording feature offered.','FlowLogs')
group([97],'1.2','workload vulnerability assessment','Inspector continuously assesses supported workloads for software vulnerabilities and unintended exposure. GuardDuty looks for threats and Config for configuration compliance; they complement but do not replace vulnerability assessment.','Inspector')
group([98],'1.2','API authorization scope','API Gateway can protect application APIs with supported authorizers, IAM authorization and other controls. IAM policies can also secure direct AWS API calls. The source does not distinguish the API type, so several options are applicable rather than uniquely wrong.','APIGateway','Review only')
group([99,110,162,218,274,284],'2.2','resource and application observability','CloudWatch provides metrics, logs, dashboards and alarms for supported resources and instrumented applications. CloudTrail records API actions and X-Ray traces requests; they offer different kinds of evidence rather than replacing general metrics/logs monitoring.','CloudWatch')
group([101,144,189,201,281],'2.1','load distribution','Elastic Load Balancing distributes traffic among registered healthy targets according to the load balancer type and configuration. Auto Scaling changes capacity rather than replacing request distribution; CloudFront caches and delivers web content.','ELB')
group([102,142],'2.2','DNS health-based failover','Route 53 health checks can influence supported routing policies to stop returning unhealthy endpoints. DNS caches and TTLs affect client recovery, so DNS failover is not instantaneous. Health checks do not replicate database data or scale tables.','Route53')
group([103],'2.2','RDS failover versus backup','RDS Multi-AZ DB instance deployments provide synchronous replication and managed standby failover. Native automated backups provide restore points separately; Multi-AZ is not itself a backup service. The practice stem asks specifically about standby replication and failover.','MultiAZ','Corrected')
group([104],'2.1','externalized recoverable state','S3 and DynamoDB can both persist application state outside a compute instance; a suitably configured cache may also retain some state but is not a universal durable authority. The required access pattern, durability and recovery semantics are missing, so there is no unique choice.','DynamoDB','Review only')
group([105,181],'2.2','Availability Zone fault tolerance','Distributing redundant application components across AZs reduces dependence on a single AZ. Sufficient remaining capacity and redundant dependencies are still required. Multi-AZ does not automatically lower global latency or data-transfer charges.','MultiAZ')
group([106,145,184,208,248],'3.4','edge content caching','CloudFront serves cacheable web content from edge locations to reduce repeated origin fetches and user latency. Cache keys, TTLs and origin design matter. It does not optimize database queries or substitute for database availability.','CloudFront')
group([107],'2.2','whole-architecture redundancy','Auto Scaling, managed databases and Elastic Beanstalk can all participate in resilient systems. No one service automatically makes every application dependency fully redundant. The source has no deployment or failure requirements that establish one unique answer.','AutoScaling','Review only')
group([108,230],'3.4','managed DNS','Route 53 is AWS\'s scalable authoritative DNS and routing service. Direct Connect and VPN provide connectivity, Global Accelerator optimizes network paths, and CloudFront delivers web content rather than replacing an authoritative DNS zone.','Route53')
group([109,227],'1.3','object version preservation','S3 Versioning retains distinct versions of objects and uses delete markers for ordinary deletes. Authorized version deletion and lifecycle rules can still permanently remove versions. Object Lock restricts deletion of protected versions rather than being the versioning feature itself.','S3Versioning')
group([111],'3.2','virtual machine compute','EC2 supplies configurable virtual server instances. Lambda executes functions, Fargate runs containers without host administration, and Glue performs data integration. These different execution models do not provide the same general-purpose instance control.','EC2')
group([112,139,147,194],'1.3','centralized backup orchestration','AWS Backup schedules, retains and restores supported resource backups, including supported EC2/EBS and hybrid workloads. Select supported resource types and configure copies explicitly. Snapshots and copied recovery points are not continuous synchronous replication.','Backup')
group([113,152,186,199],'2.2','geographic object replication','Configured S3 Cross-Region Replication copies eligible objects to a destination in another Region. Versioning and permissions are required, existing objects need an appropriate batch replication path, and replication is asynchronous rather than a zero-loss failover guarantee.','S3Replication')
group([114,117,125,141,153,179,209,236,270],'3.2','elastic EC2 capacity','EC2 Auto Scaling adjusts the instance count using configured policies and replaces unhealthy instances in the group. Metrics, health checks, bounds and AZ placement must be configured. It does not automatically scale every application dependency or replicate databases.','AutoScaling')
group([115],'2.2','cross-zone traffic distribution','Cross-zone load balancing lets a load balancer node distribute traffic to eligible targets beyond its own AZ, subject to the load balancer\'s support and configuration. The practice stem explicitly asks about targets in other AZs, which the original generic wording omitted.','ELB','Corrected')
group([116],'3.5','database migration with change capture','DMS supports full-load migration plus supported change data capture to keep a target synchronized while the source remains active. Compatibility, schema conversion and a final cutover still require planning; minimal downtime is not a universal zero-downtime guarantee.','DMS')
group([118,129,138,161],'2.2','multi-Region DynamoDB replication','DynamoDB global tables replicate a table across configured Regions and let applications use regional endpoints. Applications must handle routing and the selected consistency/conflict model; this is not automatic failover for every component of an application.','GlobalTables')
group([119,307,318,344],'4.2','interruptible spare capacity','EC2 Spot uses spare capacity at discounted rates and can be interrupted. It suits workloads designed for retry or checkpointing; it does not guarantee continuous availability, more CPU per instance, or dedicated hardware.','Spot')
group([120],'1.3','hybrid backup semantics','Storage Gateway supplies hybrid storage interfaces, while AWS Backup centrally orchestrates supported backups including supported hybrid resources. On-premises data is too broad to distinguish those roles without specifying the interface or protected resource. The source key is not uniquely justified.','Backup','Review only')
group([121,140,160,224,266],'2.2','repeatable infrastructure','CloudFormation templates define AWS resources as infrastructure as code and manage supported provisioning and updates. Templates must actually include a resilient design; using infrastructure as code alone does not create data backups or guarantee high availability.','CloudFormation')
group([122,154,178,198,213,255],'3.1','shared managed NFS storage','EFS provides elastic shared NFS file storage for supported Linux clients, including appropriately connected on-premises clients. Regional and One Zone deployments have different resilience properties. S3 is object storage and EBS is AZ-scoped block storage.','EFS')
group([123,156,176,228],'2.2','deployment versus traffic routing','Global Accelerator and Route 53 steer traffic to existing endpoints; they do not create or deploy application copies. Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation and deployment tools can provision or deploy appropriately configured applications, but the source does not specify enough to select a unique deployment service.','GlobalAccelerator','Review only')
group([124,134,155],'2.2','RDS Multi-AZ availability','A conventional RDS Multi-AZ DB instance maintains a synchronous standby in another AZ and supports managed failover for disruptions and maintenance. The single standby is not a read-scaling endpoint. Backups solve historical recovery rather than immediate standby failover.','MultiAZ')
group([126],'1.2','VPC routing without automatic failover','VPC defines the network, but a VPC does not autonomously fail over arbitrary routes or appliances. Redundant endpoints, routing and recovery mechanisms must be designed. None of these service names alone is a complete VPC failover feature.','VPC','Review only')
group([127],'3.3','serverless analytical warehouse capacity','Redshift is an analytical warehouse. Automatic demand-driven warehouse capacity is specifically a Redshift Serverless characteristic rather than a universal claim about any provisioned Redshift cluster; the practice stem adds that distinction.','Redshift','Corrected')
group([128,212,244,277],'3.4','global network acceleration','Global Accelerator directs supported traffic to healthy endpoints using the AWS global network and configured endpoint groups. It does not invariably choose the geographically nearest Region, deploy applications, or optimize SQL. The nearest-Region option is corrected to healthy endpoint routing.','GlobalAccelerator','Corrected')
group([130],'2.2','DNS-based recovery traffic switching','Route 53 failover routing can shift DNS responses to a healthy recovery endpoint, but it does not build the recovery environment or restore its data. Backups and database promotion cover different recovery steps. The practice stem explicitly asks for DNS-based endpoint failover.','Route53','Corrected')
group([131],'3.4','global content access versus arbitrary data access','CloudFront fits cacheable HTTP content, while S3, databases and file systems have other access models. Arbitrary data across geographic locations does not specify an HTTP/cacheable workload, so the source cannot uniquely select a CDN.','CloudFront','Review only')
group([132],'2.2','multi-AZ EC2 group placement','An EC2 Auto Scaling group can span configured subnets in several AZs and maintain distributed instance capacity. Placement groups address placement strategies and do not universally ensure a multi-AZ application; a purchase discount does not control AZ placement.','AutoScaling')
group([133],'3.4','global content delivery','CloudFront is a managed HTTP content delivery network with edge caching and origin integrations. Route 53 chooses DNS answers, S3 stores objects, and WAF filters web requests. They can be combined but do not perform the same content delivery role.','CloudFront')
group([135,164,188,243],'3.2','managed application environments','Elastic Beanstalk provisions and coordinates supported application environments, deployments, health monitoring and scaling. Choose a load-balanced multi-instance configuration for HA; a single-instance environment is not highly available merely because it is managed.','Beanstalk')
group([136],'2.2','healthy-target load balancing','A load balancer can send traffic to healthy redundant targets, reducing reliance on one instance. It requires multiple functioning targets and redundant dependencies. Alarms detect failures and security groups restrict traffic rather than distributing requests.','ELB')
group([137,167,205,207,226,251,264,280],'3.3','in-memory caching','ElastiCache provides managed in-memory data stores for caching and related access patterns. It can offload repeated reads and reduce latency, with suitable TTL/invalidation and availability configuration. It does not replace required authoritative database persistence or automatically increase database write throughput.','ElastiCache')
group([143,237,261],'3.3','managed key-value and document data','DynamoDB is a managed NoSQL key-value/document database. Data modeling and capacity decisions remain important for consistent low latency. Relational databases and analytical warehouses target different query and transaction models.','DynamoDB')
group([146,294],'3.1','durable object storage','S3 stores objects behind object APIs at large scale. Block volumes, file systems and databases have different access semantics. Durability, availability and retrieval behavior depend on the selected storage class and configuration.','S3')
group([148,173],'2.2','regional replication ambiguity','S3 replication, DynamoDB global tables, supported RDS cross-Region replicas and AWS Backup copies can all support different recovery needs. The question does not identify the data type or recovery mechanism, so more than one option may satisfy it.','S3Replication','Review only')
group([149],'3.2','application versions','Elastic Beanstalk application versions identify deployable application artifacts and support deploying a selected version to an environment. Scaling and health monitoring are useful environment functions but are not the version-management feature being asked about.','Beanstalk')
group([157],'2.2','Aurora global read replicas','Aurora Global Database has a primary write Region and replicated secondary clusters that serve regional reads. Write forwarding still executes writes at the primary; it is not independent low-latency multi-master writing in every Region. The source\'s read-and-write option is corrected.','AuroraGlobal','Corrected')
group([158,203],'4.1','archival object storage','The S3 Glacier storage classes serve long-term archival data with class-dependent access and restore behavior. Frequently accessed Standard storage, active shared files and relational databases do not fit the stated archival purpose. Minimum duration and retrieval fees still apply.','S3Classes')
group([159],'1.1','workforce multi-account access','IAM Identity Center, formerly AWS SSO, centrally assigns workforce access across accounts using permission sets. Organizations manages accounts and guardrail policies, not workforce sign-in permissions by itself. The source key is corrected to the offered AWS SSO option.','IdentityCenter','Corrected','c')
group([163],'2.2','health checks and routing policy','Route 53 health checks identify endpoint health, but a suitable routing configuration must consume that health state to avoid unhealthy answers. Latency and weighted routing are distinct selection mechanisms and are not by themselves outage detectors.','Route53')
group([165,260],'3.3','demand-driven managed capacity','Lambda scales event-driven execution; Aurora Serverless scales supported database compute capacity. These are service-specific forms of elasticity, not dedicated permanently assigned instances or a guarantee that every dependency scales automatically.','Lambda')
# Keep database and compute task mappings distinct for the shared elasticity idea.
REVIEW[165].update(task='3.2',ref='Lambda',topic='Lambda elasticity',reason='Lambda scales event-driven function execution without provisioning an EC2 fleet. Concurrency limits, invocation duration limits and downstream bottlenecks still apply; it does not manage application databases or guarantee dedicated instances.')
REVIEW[260].update(task='3.3',ref='Aurora',topic='Aurora Serverless capacity',reason='Aurora Serverless adjusts supported database compute capacity within configured bounds. Aurora also has availability features, but demand-driven compute scaling is the characteristic that distinguishes Serverless from provisioned Aurora.')
group([166],'2.2','obsolete or absent database answer','The offered Aurora Multi-Master name is not a current recommended substitute for conventional synchronous RDS Multi-AZ standby replication. Read replicas are usually asynchronous, Streams expose changes, and ElastiCache is a different data store. No offered current answer unambiguously satisfies the source statement.','MultiAZ','Review only')
group([168],'2.1','microservice compute ambiguity','ECS and Lambda can both run appropriately designed microservices with managed scaling. High availability depends on configuration and workload design, not merely the microservices label. Without requiring containers, neither can be dismissed as wrong.','ECS','Review only')
group([169],'3.2','cross-Region virtual-machine management','EC2 Auto Scaling groups are regional. Systems Manager can support fleet operations and CloudFormation can provision regional resources, but the source does not say whether managing means patching, provisioning, inventory or scaling. There is no unique all-Region fleet management answer.','AutoScaling','Review only')
group([170],'2.2','storage-class redundancy','Durability is a storage design property, not a named switch that ensures every S3 class spans multiple facilities. Standard and many regional classes store data across AZs; One Zone classes differ. The source lacks the storage-class scope required for its absolute claim.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([171],'2.2','redundant-target traffic routing','ELB routes requests among configured healthy targets across supported AZs. Multiple functional targets and adequate capacity are needed; CloudTrail auditing, archival storage and a hybrid network link do not replace that request routing.','ELB')
group([172],'2.2','infrastructure changes versus application deployment','CloudFormation provisions and updates declared infrastructure resources. CodeDeploy automates supported application code deployments, rather than generally managing infrastructure. The source key is corrected to CloudFormation.','CloudFormation','Corrected','a')
group([174],'2.1','queue versus pub-sub ambiguity','SQS and SNS are both managed scalable messaging services, but SQS provides a worker queue while SNS supplies pub-sub distribution. The source only says messaging system and does not specify the required delivery pattern. Both are plausible, so it is review-only.','SQS','Review only')
group([175],'3.2','scaling policy behavior','Configured scaling policies change capacity in response to demand-related signals. Launch definitions specify how instances are created, instance refresh replaces them, and scheduled actions change capacity at specified times. The practice stem clarifies metric-based scaling.','AutoScaling','Corrected')
group([177,200,216,247,272],'3.2','managed Kubernetes control plane','EKS provides a managed Kubernetes control plane. Node or Fargate capacity, cluster workload design and scaling still require configuration. ECS uses a different orchestrator, and Fargate is a compute option rather than the Kubernetes control plane itself.','EKS')
group([182],'2.2','Aurora database availability','Aurora replicates cluster storage across AZs and can fail over to appropriately configured replicas. Its HA design still needs a suitable replica topology; automatic compute scaling is a separate configuration or Serverless feature.','Aurora')
group([183],'3.3','managed relational database scope','RDS manages supported relational engines and offers backups and HA deployments. HA must be configured; EC2 hosting would require self-managed database operations and Lambda/Glue are compute/data-processing services.','RDS')
group([185],'3.5','data lake storage and governance ambiguity','Lake Formation manages permissions and governance for data lakes built on services such as S3 and the Glue catalog. S3 supplies the durable storage. The source conflates an entire lake with its governance service and does not distinguish the storage layer.','LakeFormation','Review only')
group([187,191,233],'3.2','container orchestration','ECS orchestrates container services and tasks. EKS also orchestrates containers using Kubernetes, so a generic Docker question with both options is not unique. Where Kubernetes is not offered and managed orchestration is explicit, ECS is the intended best fit.','ECS')
REVIEW[187]['status']='Review only';REVIEW[187]['reason']='Both ECS and EKS orchestrate container workloads; Docker packaging alone does not distinguish them. AWS Batch also runs containerized batch jobs, while Lambda runs event handlers. The source requires an orchestration or workload requirement before one answer can be uniquely graded.'
group([190,245,278],'3.3','analytical warehouse','Redshift is a managed analytical data warehouse for large SQL workloads. S3 is object storage, Glue performs integration/cataloging, and OLTP databases target different transactional access patterns. Choose capacity and data layout for the workload.','Redshift')
group([192],'2.1','load balancing primary purpose','ELB distributes requests among registered targets. TLS termination is a supported additional capability for some load balancers, but it is not the primary load-distribution role. Snapshots and dependency management are separate functions.','ELB')
group([193,258],'2.2','native S3 durability versus extra replication','S3 storage classes already define native durability and availability behavior; Cross-Region Replication adds a configured remote copy. It is not required to make every S3 object durable. The source wording confuses baseline storage redundancy with optional regional recovery.','S3Replication','Review only')
group([195],'3.2','scaling and balancing are separate','Auto Scaling adjusts capacity; ELB distributes requests. They integrate but Auto Scaling alone is not the load balancer. The practice stem explicitly asks for the service that adjusts capacity while integrated with ELB.','AutoScaling','Corrected')
group([196],'2.2','Aurora replicated storage','Aurora\'s cluster storage replication across AZs supports durability and availability. Global Database is an optional cross-Region feature and instance scaling addresses capacity rather than being the fundamental storage redundancy mechanism.','Aurora')
group([197],'3.3','multiple valid managed relational services','RDS and Aurora both provide managed relational databases and HA options. The generic wording does not distinguish an RDS-supported engine requirement from Aurora compatibility. Redshift is an analytical warehouse and DynamoDB is NoSQL, but RDS versus Aurora is not uniquely settled.','RDS','Review only')
group([202,273],'3.3','read replica offload','RDS read replicas serve eligible read queries and typically replicate asynchronously. They do not increase the primary\'s write capacity, and replica lag affects freshness. Promotion and cross-Region replicas can help recovery, but read scaling is their main distinguishing purpose here.','ReadReplicas')
group([204],'3.3','database bottleneck ambiguity','A larger instance can address compute or memory limits, while read replicas can address read-heavy pressure. Multi-AZ targets availability and monitoring diagnoses the bottleneck. Without workload measurements, both scaling choices can improve performance.','ReadReplicas','Review only')
group([206,257,292],'3.2','container platform ambiguity','ECS schedules container tasks; Fargate supplies serverless capacity for ECS and eligible EKS workloads; EKS supplies Kubernetes orchestration. More than one offered option can run Docker containers. The source needs an orchestrator or server-management constraint for a unique answer.','ECS','Review only')
group([210],'4.1','lifecycle versus access-based tiering','Lifecycle rules transition objects based on configured age or filters, not by individually observing last-access frequency. Intelligent-Tiering monitors eligible object access and moves objects between its tiers. The source asks for access-pattern automation but does not offer that direct feature.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([211],'2.2','application disaster-recovery scope','Multi-AZ DB instances handle database AZ failover, Route 53 handles traffic failover, Backup handles recovery points, and CloudFormation can recreate resources. Application HA and regional disaster recovery require a coordinated design; the generic source has no unique service answer.','MultiAZ','Review only')
group([214],'3.2','compute-optimized instances','EC2 C-family instances are compute optimized for CPU-intensive workloads. T-family instances are burstable, M-family instances are general purpose, and R-family instances are memory optimized. Benchmark actual requirements rather than selecting only by family name.','EC2')
group([215],'3.3','DynamoDB read bottleneck ambiguity','Higher read capacity helps when provisioned capacity is insufficient, caching can reduce repeated reads, and global tables can reduce regional distance for distributed users. Streams expose changes rather than increasing read capacity. The missing access pattern prevents a unique performance recommendation.','DynamoDB','Review only')
group([217],'3.5','accelerated S3 transfers','S3 Transfer Acceleration can improve supported long-distance transfers using edge-network paths. It does not reduce object storage charges or guarantee that every transfer is faster; measure the source-to-bucket path and consider its additional transfer charges.','S3Acceleration')
group([219,279],'4.2','Reserved Instance baseline discounts','Matching Reserved Instances can reduce eligible EC2 compute charges for predictable long-term usage. On-Demand avoids commitment, Spot tolerates interruptions, and Dedicated Hosts address host-control/licensing needs. A reservation is not the same as universally reserving capacity in every scope.','RI')
group([220],'3.1','file-system requirements missing','EFS is shared NFS storage and FSx for Windows supplies managed SMB files; either may be high performance for the relevant clients. The source omits protocol, operating system and throughput requirements, so it cannot declare one universally best distributed file system.','EFS','Review only')
group([221],'2.2','cross-Region replication versus failover','A supported RDS cross-Region read replica supplies an asynchronous remote copy but does not promise automatic cross-Region failover for a conventional RDS DB instance. Multi-AZ remains within one Region. The source\'s ensure-HA language requires a promotion and traffic recovery design.','ReadReplicas','Review only')
group([222],'3.1','unstructured object data','S3 is appropriate for large collections of frequently accessed unstructured objects when an object interface is required. EFS is appropriate for shared file semantics instead, so the practice stem adds the missing object-API requirement.','S3','Corrected')
group([223],'3.4','latency-based DNS routing','Route 53 latency routing uses AWS latency measurements to select a configured Region associated with a latency record. It does not mean simple geographical proximity or guarantee the lowest instantaneous latency to every arbitrary endpoint.','Route53')
group([225],'3.5','stream ingestion versus analytics','Kinesis Data Streams retains and distributes stream records; applications or analytics engines process those records. Redshift, Athena and QuickSight have distinct analytical uses, but a stream alone is not an engine providing automatic real-time analytics. The source conflates ingestion with computation.','Kinesis','Review only')
group([229],'3.3','DynamoDB capacity-change ambiguity','Increasing provisioned write capacity and configuring provisioned-capacity auto scaling can both increase supported traffic capacity. Streams capture changes and global tables add regional copies. The source omits whether traffic is reads, writes, bursty or predictable.','DynamoDB','Review only')
group([231],'3.5','serverless S3 SQL queries','Athena queries supported data in S3 using SQL without managing a query cluster. Glue catalog metadata, permissions and suitable file layouts are still required. Redshift Spectrum and EMR can also query S3, so the practice stem specifies the serverless direct-query pattern.','Athena','Corrected')
group([232],'3.1','parallel Lustre storage','FSx for Lustre provides a managed parallel file system for high-performance computing and related large-scale processing. Windows SMB, passive archive storage and backup orchestration are different workloads and interfaces.','Lustre')
group([234],'2.2','capacity replacement versus request balancing','ELB can route among healthy instances across AZs, while Auto Scaling can replace or expand instances. Both can improve availability; the source does not specify whether the problem is traffic distribution or replacement capacity.','ELB','Review only')
group([235],'3.3','MySQL-compatible alternatives','RDS for MySQL and Aurora MySQL can both support managed MySQL-compatible databases with appropriate HA configuration. The source does not require the community MySQL engine specifically or distinguish Aurora compatibility, so both choices are valid.','RDS','Review only')
group([238],'3.4','latency depends on the access path','Transfer Acceleration targets supported long-distance S3 transfers, not a blanket guarantee of low-latency application reads. CloudFront caching, regional placement and request patterns matter. The source lacks a geographic transfer condition and does not offer CloudFront.','S3Acceleration','Review only')
group([240,241,290],'2.2','log query analysis','CloudWatch Logs collects application/resource logs, and Logs Insights runs interactive queries over selected logs. It is not a general streaming-event processing engine. Logs, permissions and relevant time ranges must exist before they can be queried.','CloudWatchLogs')
group([242],'4.1','automatic access tiering','S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves eligible objects between access tiers based on observed access, with optional archival tiers requiring activation. Glacier storage classes do not independently discover and automatically move arbitrary objects into themselves. The source key is corrected to Intelligent-Tiering.','S3Classes','Corrected','d')
group([246],'2.2','regional Aurora redundancy','Aurora cluster storage spans AZs and a configured replica topology supports regional failover. Global Database adds cross-Region replication but is optional, while backups address restoration. The source key is corrected to the Multi-AZ choice and the practice wording specifies regional cluster availability.','Aurora','Corrected','a')
group([249],'1.3','two valid RDS backup mechanisms','Native RDS automated backups and AWS Backup can both automate supported RDS recovery points and restoration workflows. The source does not specify native point-in-time recovery or centralized policy management, so neither offered service can be universally dismissed.','Backup','Review only')
group([252],'4.2','interruptible workload savings','Spot capacity can reduce EC2 spending for short-term jobs that tolerate interruption and retry. On-Demand has no commitment but higher baseline rates, Reserved Instances require a matching commitment, and Dedicated Hosts target host-level requirements rather than cheap transient jobs.','Spot')
group([253],'3.2','managed machine learning','SageMaker AI provides managed capabilities for building, training and deploying ML models. Lambda and EC2 can execute custom workloads but do not by themselves supply this managed ML lifecycle. Glue is data integration.','SageMaker')
group([254],'3.4','weighted DNS selection','Route 53 weighted routing selects among records according to configured relative weights, subject to supported health handling. Equal weights are only one special case; this policy is not the same as latency routing or primary/secondary failover.','Route53')
group([256,291],'3.4','global routing alternatives','Global Accelerator optimizes supported network paths to configured healthy endpoints; Route 53 latency routing chooses DNS answers using latency measurements; CloudFront serves HTTP content from edges. The source does not specify protocol, static IPs or caching, so more than one offered approach is valid.','GlobalAccelerator','Review only')
group([259,282],'3.2','serverless execution models','Lambda is event-driven serverless compute, while Fargate is serverless compute for containers. A question asking only for serverless applications without a function or event requirement does not distinguish them. ECS on Fargate may also be a suitable application platform.','Lambda','Review only')
group([262],'3.5','legacy streaming analytics name','The former Kinesis Data Analytics for Apache Flink is now Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink; the separate SQL Applications offering was discontinued. The practice option is modernized to Managed Service for Apache Flink for stateful streaming analytics.','Flink','Corrected')
group([263],'3.2','serverless containers','ECS with Fargate runs managed container tasks without administering EC2 worker hosts. Service scaling policies must still be configured. EKS alone does not imply serverless worker capacity and Lambda is a different execution model.','ECS')
group([265,285],'2.2','multi-tier availability ambiguity','ELB plus Auto Scaling addresses web/compute distribution and replacement, while Multi-AZ RDS addresses database failover and Route 53 can address endpoint recovery. A multi-tier design needs appropriate resilience in every tier; the source does not isolate the tier being fixed.','ELB','Review only')
group([267,342],'4.1','automatic object tier optimization','Intelligent-Tiering monitors eligible S3 object access and moves data among its access tiers. Optional archive tiers are configured separately. It does not mean every object is always in the globally cheapest class regardless of retrieval, object size or monitoring charges.','S3Classes')
group([269],'2.2','Beanstalk scaling and load distribution','A load-balanced Elastic Beanstalk environment coordinates an Auto Scaling group and load balancer for application traffic and capacity. A single-instance environment does not supply equivalent redundancy; logs and encryption address other concerns.','Beanstalk')
group([271,289],'2.2','out-of-scope deployment material','CodeDeploy automates supported application deployments, CodePipeline orchestrates delivery stages, and CloudFormation provisions infrastructure. The source answer is understandable for direct application deployment, but CodeDeploy is listed outside SAA-C03 scope. This item remains available for reference but not scored SAA practice.','CodeDeploy','Review only')
group([275],'3.4','cached S3 content near viewers','CloudFront can cache HTTP-served S3 objects near viewers and reduce repeated origin round trips. Transfer Acceleration speeds supported S3 transfer paths rather than providing a viewer content cache; versioning and storage tiering address different concerns.','CloudFront')
group([283],'3.2','Fargate host administration','Fargate runs containers without customers provisioning and patching the underlying EC2 hosts. ECS service auto scaling is a separately configured capability; Fargate alone does not automatically resize every application or optimize network traffic.','Fargate')
group([286],'4.1','archival tier versus automation','Glacier classes provide archival object storage, while Intelligent-Tiering can automate movement to optional archive tiers. The original mixes archival storage and automatic archival, and the generic Glacier name omits restore characteristics. No uniquely best class can be inferred.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([287],'3.2','event-driven function compute','Lambda executes function code in response to events or invocations without managing a server fleet. Container orchestration and durable storage are other functions; an entire scalable web application can use Lambda but also needs its API and state architecture.','Lambda')
group([288],'3.5','retained event streams','Kinesis Data Streams ingests and retains ordered records within shards for consumer applications. S3 stores objects and DynamoDB/RDS are databases; although databases can expose change streams, they are not the dedicated retained event-stream service offered.','Kinesis')
group([293],'1.2','web request protection','WAF applies request filtering rules to supported web endpoints to mitigate common web exploits. It is not application telemetry, content acceleration or backup management. Request rules should complement secure code and be tested for false positives.','WAF')
group([295],'2.2','redundant compute architecture','Auto Scaling and ELB can keep a configured redundant EC2 tier available through target failure and load variation. Backups protect recoverability rather than live compute capacity. The practice stem narrows the original generic application to the EC2 tier.','ELB','Corrected')
group([296],'3.1','managed Windows SMB files','FSx for Windows File Server offers managed SMB storage and Windows/Active Directory integration. EFS is NFS, S3 is object storage and EBS is block storage; those interfaces are not interchangeable for an unchanged SMB application.','FSxWindows')
group([297],'3.5','offline physical data transfer','Snowball historically moved large datasets using shipped devices rather than a live stream. It is not open to new customers, so this legacy-service item is preserved for reference and excluded from current scored practice. Online alternatives depend on bandwidth and migration needs.','Snowball','Review only')
group([298],'2.1','API type ambiguity','API Gateway manages supported REST, HTTP and WebSocket APIs; AppSync manages GraphQL APIs. Without an API style or protocol requirement, both are plausible. Lambda supplies handlers and CloudFront delivery, but the source does not distinguish the two API management services.','APIGateway','Review only')
group([300],'3.3','database model and scaling ambiguity','RDS, DynamoDB, Aurora and Redshift all provide managed databases with distinct backup and scaling capabilities. The source omits relational versus key-value versus analytical requirements and the intended scaling mode, so it cannot uniquely justify RDS.','RDS','Review only')
group([301,331],'4.2','metered function execution','Lambda charges eligible execution primarily by requests and duration/resource allocation rather than requiring a long-term instance commitment. Optional provisioned capacity adds charges. Spot and On-Demand EC2 meter instance capacity rather than providing the same per-function invocation execution model.','Lambda')
group([302,330,355,374,382],'4.2','Spot suitability requires interruption tolerance','Spot may be inexpensive but capacity can be reclaimed. Variable or short-lived demand alone does not establish interruption tolerance. On-Demand avoids commitments and Spot interruption risk; the original needs recovery and availability requirements before one can be called best.','Spot','Review only')
group([303],'4.1','multiple pay-as-you-go storage services','S3, EBS and S3 Glacier classes all have usage-based storage charging without an ordinary long-term purchase commitment. Their object/block interfaces and access latency differ. The generic pay-as-you-go requirement does not uniquely select S3.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([304],'4.3','variable database workload model','DynamoDB on-demand or provisioned auto scaling can suit variable key-value demand, while Aurora Serverless can suit variable relational workloads. Generic variable database demand does not establish the data model or capacity mode, so DynamoDB is not uniquely best as stated.','DynamoDB','Review only')
group([305,320,323,335,340,350,363,380,387],'4.2','commitment discounts overlap','Reserved Instances and Savings Plans both offer eligible usage discounts for one- or three-year commitments. Choice depends on configuration flexibility, eligible service, commitment utilization and pricing. The source does not distinguish them, and a fixed maximum-savings percentage is not a universal current rate.','SavingsPlans','Review only')
group([306,321,336,345,360,383,388,396],'4.2','interactive cost analysis','Cost Explorer provides interactive cost and usage views, grouping, forecasts and supported purchase recommendations. CUR provides detailed exported records, Budgets monitors thresholds, and Trusted Advisor provides best-practice checks. Cost Explorer does not automatically make every resource cheaper.','CostExplorer')
group([308],'4.1','generic Glacier family label','The listed generic S3 Glacier name denotes archival storage but does not distinguish Instant Retrieval, Flexible Retrieval or Deep Archive. Minimum retention, retrieval requirements and data size affect total cost. It is an archival choice but not enough information for an absolute most-cost-effective claim.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([309],'4.2','pre-deployment estimates','AWS Pricing Calculator estimates planned service usage before deployment. Cost Explorer and CUR analyze actual recorded usage, while Budgets compares tracked costs/usage against thresholds. Estimates depend on inputs and do not guarantee the final invoice.','PricingCalculator')
group([310,361],'4.2','scaling down unused capacity','Configured scaling or scheduled actions can reduce unneeded EC2 capacity when demand falls. An Auto Scaling group must have suitable minimum capacity and policies; it does not automatically detect and stop every idle instance in the account. The practice stem makes the group/configuration condition explicit.','AutoScaling','Corrected')
group([311,354,371,386,392],'4.2','cost-oriented best-practice checks','Trusted Advisor checks supported resource configurations for issues including certain idle or underused resources and makes recommendations. It does not automatically delete every unused resource. Cost Explorer/Compute Optimizer can supply complementary evidence.','TrustedAdvisor')
group([312,324],'4.1','frequent-access object storage','S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed regional object data with multi-AZ resilience. Archival classes add access trade-offs and One Zone-IA trades away AZ redundancy and adds infrequent-access charging. Actual total cost still depends on request and transfer patterns.','S3Classes')
group([313,338,358,369,389],'4.2','cost and usage threshold alerts','AWS Budgets monitors configured cost or usage budgets and sends threshold/forecast notifications. Optional supported budget actions can be configured separately. Budgets is not a universal hard spending cap and does not prevent every unexpected charge.','Budgets')
group([314],'4.4','internet delivery versus dedicated hybrid transport','Direct Connect serves a dedicated hybrid circuit, not a generic replacement for VPC internet traffic. CloudFront may reduce origin traffic for cacheable content; accelerators can add fees. The required destination and volume are missing, so no listed service guarantees lower internet transfer costs.','DirectConnect','Review only')
group([315,339],'4.4','endpoint costs depend on traffic path','S3/DynamoDB gateway endpoints can avoid NAT processing fees for matching regional traffic. PrivateLink interface endpoints have their own charges; Transit Gateway adds attachment/processing charges. None universally lowers all regional/service transfer costs without a specified traffic path.','Endpoint','Review only')
group([316,333,353,370,385],'4.1','infrequent-access resilience requirements','One Zone-IA supplies millisecond object access at reduced storage cost for an appropriate infrequent-access workload, but stores data in one AZ and charges retrieval/minimum durations. Without allowing loss of that AZ copy or specifying recreatable data, it is not universally the correct low-cost choice.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([317],'4.1','snapshot lifecycle tool mismatch','Data Lifecycle Manager automates retention and deletion of snapshots it manages. Systems Manager can execute custom automation but is not a built-in universal unused-snapshot detector. The named EBS Snapshot Manager is not the offered official feature name; no source choice precisely matches the claim.','DLM','Review only')
group([319,399],'4.2','detailed billing export','AWS Cost and Usage Reports provide detailed line-item billing and usage data, including supported resource and activated cost allocation tag detail. Reports enable downstream analysis but are not the same as a native interactive visualization or a budget alert mechanism.','CUR')
group([322],'4.2','Lambda pricing versus management tool','Lambda pricing describes metering of requests and execution duration; it is not a separate AWS cost-management service. Cost Explorer analyzes spending and Budgets alerts on it. The source conflates a pricing schedule with a management service and has no uniquely correct key.','Lambda','Review only')
group([325],'4.2','configuration inventory','Config records supported resource configurations and their history, which can inform resource governance and cost investigations. It is not the detailed billing dataset; Cost Explorer and CUR provide financial usage evidence.','Config')
group([326,365,378],'4.2','right-sized instance count','Configured Auto Scaling changes instance count to match capacity demand and can reduce idle instance-hours. Discounts change unit prices rather than the number of resources. Policies, minimum bounds and workload metrics must be configured.','AutoScaling')
group([327,329,334,347,367],'4.2','cost datasets versus analysis overlap','CUR offers detailed billing exports and Cost Explorer also tracks and analyzes cost and usage across supported accounts and Regions. The source does not require a line-item export, so both can satisfy the generic cost-analysis wording. Budgets instead supplies thresholds and Advisor supplies checks.','CUR','Review only')
group([328,359,364,390,391,400],'4.1','archive retrieval requirements omitted','Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Deep Archive have different restore times, minimum storage durations and request/retrieval charges; Standard-IA can be appropriate when immediate reads are needed. Occasional access alone cannot establish the most cost-effective class without retention, size and restore requirements.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([332,376,398],'4.2','recommendation tool overlap','Trusted Advisor provides selected resource optimization checks; Cost Explorer provides supported rightsizing and commitment purchase recommendations based on usage. Both can contribute to the requested cost recommendations, so the generic source does not establish a unique service.','CostExplorer','Review only')
group([337,356,375],'4.1','long-duration cold archival tier','S3 Glacier Deep Archive targets very rarely retrieved, long-retained objects when asynchronous restoration is acceptable. It offers low storage pricing but minimum duration and retrieval costs apply. It is not suitable when millisecond access is required.','S3Classes')
group([341],'2.2','economical multi-AZ compute','Configured Auto Scaling across AZs plus request distribution can maintain redundant capacity without running peak capacity at all times. Spot interruptions and purchase discounts do not themselves guarantee HA. The practice option explicitly includes a load balancer and sufficient capacity.','AutoScaling','Corrected')
group([343,351,372,381,397],'4.4','CDN delivery and origin offload','CloudFront caches eligible HTTP content near viewers and can reduce origin fetches while improving latency. Global Accelerator optimizes network paths without caching, Direct Connect is hybrid connectivity, and S3 Transfer Acceleration targets transfer paths. Total CDN cost still depends on traffic and cacheability.','CloudFront')
group([346],'4.1','restore latency is unspecified','Generic S3 Glacier could mean several classes with very different retrieval times; S3 Standard and active Intelligent-Tiering tiers give immediate reads. Low retrieval time and affordable backup require explicit latency, retention and access-volume constraints, so the source key cannot be uniquely justified.','S3Classes','Review only')
group([348],'4.3','on-demand key-value capacity','DynamoDB on-demand capacity can accommodate variable key-value/document traffic without buying a fixed instance fleet or configuring provisioned capacity in advance. It is not a drop-in relational database substitute, and always-lowest cost is not guaranteed. The practice stem states the key-value requirement.','DynamoDB','Corrected')
group([349,357,384],'4.2','horizontal scaling versus instance resizing','EC2 Auto Scaling primarily changes fleet instance count and replaces instances; it does not simply resize running instances vertically according to demand. Right-sizing uses measured resource needs and planned replacements. The practice wording is corrected to instance-count scaling.','AutoScaling','Corrected')
group([352],'4.2','analysis is not automatic cost reduction','Cost Explorer can expose Lambda usage costs, timeouts can bound runaway execution, and Budgets can warn about spending. None alone is universally most cost-effective. Provisioned concurrency adds cost and is chosen for latency requirements, not automatically for cost reduction.','Lambda','Review only')
group([362,377,394],'3.5','data migration depends on available transport','Snowball historically provided offline transfer; DataSync automates online storage transfer; Direct Connect supplies a link; Transfer Family provides managed file-transfer protocols. Dataset size, bandwidth, deadlines and current service eligibility are missing, so Snowball cannot be called universally best.','Snowball','Review only')
group([366,379],'4.2','tagging versus billing activation','Tag Editor can add supported resource tags. To use tags for billing attribution, activate eligible cost allocation tags through the billing workflow; creating a tag alone does not activate it for cost reports. The practice stem explicitly asks only for bulk application of resource tags.','TagEditor','Corrected')
group([368,395],'4.2','uncommitted noninterruptible compute','On-Demand EC2 avoids long-term purchase commitments and interruption due to Spot reclamation. It does not guarantee unlimited available capacity, so capacity planning can still be needed. The practice stem clarifies that Spot interruptions are unacceptable.','EC2','Corrected')
group([373],'4.2','analysis and recommendation ambiguity','Cost Explorer offers billing analysis and purchase/rightsizing recommendations, while Trusted Advisor offers supported configuration cost checks. The source does not specify financial trend analysis versus resource checks, so both are applicable.','CostExplorer','Review only')
group([393],'4.2','tag-based allocation','Activated cost allocation tags attribute supported billing records to projects or departments. Explorer and CUR can consume that metadata, while Budgets monitors thresholds. Tags must be applied consistently and activated for billing rather than merely created.','CostTags')
